## Canonical context (read before any non-trivial change)

1. `.workforce/intent.md` — the project's falsifiable goal. If missing, intent hasn't been captured; recommend a Claude Code session with `/intent-validator`.
2. `.workforce/alignment-report.md` — latest drift status (`PASS` / `PASS-WITH-NOTES` / `BLOCK`). A `BLOCK` halts orchestration; address required actions before architectural changes.
3. `docs/claude/architecture.md` — system architecture (components, data flows, security boundaries).
4. `docs/claude/development.md` — how to run, test, deploy.
5. `docs/claude/design-decisions.md` — why the stack is what it is.
6. `docs/decisions/` — Architecture Decision Records.

## Behavioral rules

1. Read `.workforce/intent.md` before any architectural change.
2. Use the env var prefix `{{ env_prefix }}_` for all application env vars.
3. Never hard-code credentials. They live in `.env` (gitignored).
4. Prefer editing existing files to creating new ones.
5. Don't add features, refactor, or introduce abstractions beyond what the task requires.

## When to switch to Claude Code

If the user asks for a multi-engineer dispatch, an audit, a greenfield scaffold, or an alignment check, recommend running `/foundation`, `/workforce`, `/sync`, `/perf`, or one of the orchestrator agents (`intent-validator`, `conductor`, `alignment-guard`) in Claude Code.

Codex is the right tool for in-session refinement, debugging, and tight-loop coding work. Orchestration lives in Claude Code.

